{
  "case": "retry-paraphrase",
  "question": "How often may a temporary network error be attempted again?",
  "context": {},
  "finish": {
    "action": "finish",
    "paths": [
      "topics/r/9a2b24a7-3464-4ce4-b488-28e5c6de71d3-v1.md"
    ],
    "complete": false,
    "clarification_required": false,
    "rationale": "Answer: retry a temporary (transient) request error up to three times, with exponential backoff between attempts. Keep the same request identifier on every attempt. Don't retry a permanent validation failure; return it immediately. Source: 'Bounded retries' (topics/r/9a2b24a7-\u2026-v1.md, sha256 1b70a9df\u2026). It passed every current-record gate: active, repository scope, no conditions, trust reviewed, visible, and nothing active replaces it. Provenance: a fixture-observer executable test (fixture://quality/retry-limit). Its admission reason says it is an authored synthetic assertion, so memory is not independent proof, and this should be checked against the current code before it is used for anything consequential. Related but not part of the answer: 'Revised deadline' v2 is the corrected version of v1 and puts the confirmed request deadline at sixty seconds. It doesn't say how that deadline interacts with retries. The two 'Idempotency receipt' records say a repeated request key returns the first successful response, which supports keeping the request ID across retries, but they don't answer how many times to retry. I found no contradictory retry rule among the active repository-scoped sources. I skipped the superseded and revoked records ('Legacytimeout' v1, 'Retired protocol', 'Withdrawn fallback'). Coverage is incomplete: the sockets and DNS leaves need component:src/sockets or component:src/dns context, and none was supplied, so I didn't expand them. The reader also excluded 978 records for missing component context. Those could hold component-specific retry rules that override this one for socket or DNS errors. I have not shown that no such rule exists."
  },
